  "finding": "`ixor_gf2_mul` is the Karatsuba recursion to the gate: 63 static Toffolis at GF(2^12), matching K(12) = 63, and matching at every degree measured (GF(2^2)=3, GF(2^3)=7, GF(2^4)=9, GF(2^6)=21, GF(2^8)=27, GF(2^12)=63). No split of the recursion beats it at m=12: the best is 63, schoolbook is 144. Gating is invisible in the emitted stream -- six levers emit 3,486,597 static Toffolis against the reference's 5,725,461 (1.642x), and the quotient gate changes that by exactly 0, because it adds conditions rather than removing gates; the whole static saving is `clear_eea`, which deletes the reverse EEA (5,725,461 vs five levers 3,486,597). A local peephole removes 0 Toffolis across both arms, so there is no adjacent-cancellation headroom. The sweep's multiply is 59.48% of the six-lever arm's executed Toffolis (788,250.0 of 1,325,230.0 per shot, 10,510 calls), which makes it the largest remaining lever but not the only one: a hypothetical 45-AND multiply would give 5.039x, and a multiply that cost nothing but its 12-Toffoli controlled add would give 8.634x, still above Benchmark II's 445,229. No such cheaper coherent multiply was in hand when this was recorded; a free one requires measuring an operand, which `docs/CIRCUIT_OPTIMISATION_RULES.md` rules inadmissible."
